// Temporal (session-aware) authorization for refunds (Lab 5, Dogwood).
// Dogwood is a superset of Cedar; every Cedar policy is valid Dogwood. This
// file holds the one rule that needs session history.
//
// The ONLY permit for process_refund lives here, so a refund is allowed only
// when BOTH hold: the caller acts as their own identity, AND they already
// looked up THE SAME order earlier in this session. No prior lookup => no
// permit => deny-by-default. The $200 cap (forbid in refund.cedar) still wins.
//
// Requires: the caller sends a policy session id on every request via the
// `x-amzn-bedrock-agentcore-policy-session-id` header (the tester reuses its
// conversation session id), and the Gateway IAM role has
// `bedrock-agentcore:GetWorkloadAccessToken`. Temporal policies are supported
// in eu-central-1 (Frankfurt).
//
// Replace <GATEWAY_ARN> and confirm the target name (`payment`) and the
// "username" tag, same as refund.cedar.

permit (
    principal is AgentCore::OAuthUser,
    action == AgentCore::Action::"payment___process_refund",
    resource == AgentCore::Gateway::"<GATEWAY_ARN>"
)
when {
    context.input.customer == principal.getTag("username")
}
when temporal {
    formerly within 1h AgentCore::Action::"payment___lookup_order"::response{
        input.customer: context.input.customer,
        input.order_id: context.input.order_id
    }
};

// Further temporal patterns (see the Dogwood language guide for exact syntax):
//   - per-session refund cap: forbid process_refund  when temporal { count ... }
//   - running total limit:    forbid process_refund  when temporal { sum ... }
// If the console rejects the combined `when { } when temporal { }` above, move
// the identity check into a separate forbid (forbid unless customer == tag).
